 "regions": [
  {
   "id": "r2",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "engaged in pleasing the assembly of the wise, and arranges",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "words with care agreeably to popular taste.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r3",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "17. Good men should be waited upon, though they may",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "not give good advice (in a special way). For what come from",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "them as random discourses serve as rules for guidance.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r4",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "18. Though falling, a noble man generally falls like a",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "ball (i. e. to rise again); but a mean fellow falls like a lump",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "of clay ( never to rise again ).",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r5",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "19. If ever by chance the world became destitute of",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "lotuses, would the swan then, like a cock, scratch a dunghill?",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r6",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "20. That elephants whose temples are split open by ( the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "line of) rut stand lazy through drowsiness, at the door, as",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "also steeds decked with gold ornaments neigh proudly, and",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "that one is awakened from sleep by the sound of lutes,",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 5,
     "text": "clarionets, drums, conches, and tabors:—all that, like ( the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 6,
     "text": "accompaniments) of the lord of heaven, is the manifestation",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 7,
     "text": "of the power of religious merit.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r7",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "2I. Redness in lotuses, beneficence in goodmen, and ruth-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "lessness in the wicked—these three are by nature established",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "respectively in the three.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r8",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "22. True speech is the highest ornament for man;modesty",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "for a woman and slenderness of the middle; ( with the read-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "ing गजा०-for a woman with the stately gait of an elephant",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "slenderness &c. ); and for the twice-born, learning and in",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 5,
     "text": "addition patience. Good character is an ornament for all men.",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
  {
   "id": "r9",
   "type": "translated_stanza",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "23. Beloved one, wicked fate, having, like a skilful",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "potter, per force rolled our mind into a ball of clay, as it",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "were, whirls it round and round by placing it on the wheel",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "of anxiety which revolves being driven by a series of strokes",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 5,
     "text": "from the rod in the form of adversity; we know not what",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 6,
     "text": "ate will do !",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": true,
   "note": "Line 6 reads 'ate', likely a broken 'f' in 'fate'."
  },
